parameters.text:
=You are a senior SEO strategist deciding whether a business should target a specific keyword. Be decisive and commercially realistic, not just technically correct.

PAGE DETAILS
Target Keyword: {{ $json.keyword }}
Language: {{ $json.language_name || 'English' }}
Business: {{ $json.business || 'Not provided' }}
Target Audience: {{ $json.audience || 'Not provided' }}
Website: {{ $json.domain || 'Not provided' }}
Target Country: {{ $json.country }}
Intended Page Type: {{ $json.page_type }}
Business goal: {{ $json.goal || 'leads' }}
{{ $json.existing_page ? 'THE SITE ALREADY HAS A PAGE FOR THIS SEARCH: ' + $json.existing_page.url + ' — the plan is to IMPROVE that page. Never recommend a new page or a second URL for this keyword.' : 'No existing page on this topic was found on the site.' }}

{{ $json.own_coverage_text ? 'WHAT THE SITE ALREADY HAS ON THIS TOPIC (its own pages, keyword ladders and drafts written but not yet published):\n' + $json.own_coverage_text + '\nNever recommend building a page one of these already is, never give their searches as secondary_keywords for this page, and never ask for a full section on a topic one of them covers: name it and link to it instead.' : '' }}

{{ $json.existing_page_facts_text ? 'WHAT THE CLIENT\'S PAGE HAS (measured on the whole page; the only source for any statement about what that page contains or lacks):\n' + $json.existing_page_facts_text : '' }}

SITE AUTHORITY (the client's site today)
{{ $json.site_authority ? JSON.stringify($json.site_authority) : 'unknown — no domain given or no data' }}

SEARCH RESULTS PAGE FEATURES
{{ $json.serp_digest || 'n/a' }}

KEYWORD DATA
{{ JSON.stringify($json.keyword_data, null, 2) }}

COMPETITOR ANALYSIS
{{ JSON.stringify($json.competitor_analysis, null, 2) }}

COMPETITOR PAGES SUMMARY
{{ JSON.stringify($json.competitors_summary, null, 2) }}

TASK
Weigh search volume against ranking effort, keyword difficulty and top-10 link strength against the site's authority, search intent against the intended page type, SERP features (AI Overview, snippets, video) against the clicks left for organic results, and competitor strength against realistic upside for the business goal. Flag any red flags (near-zero volume, mismatched intent, dominant unbeatable competitors, low commercial value for a business page).

Return ONLY valid JSON (no markdown, no backticks, no extra text) in this exact structure:
{
  "verdict": "GO" | "GO_WITH_CHANGES" | "AVOID",
  "score": 0-100,
  "reasons": ["2-4 short, specific reasons that quote the actual numbers above"],
  "risks": ["0-3 risks or watch-outs; empty array if none"],
  "recommended_page": "best-fit page type, e.g. Service Page / Blog Post / Comparison Page / Landing Page / Guide",
  "what_must_change": ["for GO_WITH_CHANGES: the concrete changes (page type, angle, targeting, supporting content); else empty"],
  "topics_to_skip": ["2-5 word noun phrases for subtopics or questions this page must NOT cover because they do not match the buyer's intent (e.g. \"consultancy licence\", \"starting an IT company\"); empty when there are none. Every topic you tell the writer to skip in what_must_change goes here too."],
  "expected_monthly_visits_top3": 0,
  "time_to_rank_months": 0,
  "secondary_keywords": ["6-10 related keywords worth targeting on the same page"]
}

RULES
- Use AVOID only when the fit is clearly poor: unrealistic ranking odds, wrong intent, or dominant competitors with no content gap to exploit.
- Use GO_WITH_CHANGES when the keyword is workable but the page type, angle, or targeting should change first.
- reasons and risks must reference the actual numbers/data above, not generic SEO advice.
- An AI Overview, a featured snippet or any other results-page feature is ONE observation at ONE time (the check time is in the SEARCH RESULTS PAGE FEATURES block). Write "an AI Overview was seen in the check at …" or "no AI Overview was seen in the check at …", never "there is no AI Overview" or "the AI Overview takes the clicks" as a fact; AI Overviews vary between searches, so the verdict and the score must not hinge on that one observation.
- When a page of the site is named above as already answering this search, every recommendation is about THAT URL: `recommended_page` is the page type that URL should become, `what_must_change` starts with what to change on it (name the URL), and nothing may suggest publishing a new page, a second URL, a "dedicated" or "separate" page for the keyword. Two pages for one search split the signals and neither ranks.
- Never ask to "add" a figure, a date or a threshold the client's page already states: ask only for what it does not carry.
- Never state as a fact that the client's own page lacks something (dates, tables, an author, a publication date, a call to action, a section). The only source for that is the PAGE FACTS block above: say a thing is missing only when it says "none found", and say nothing when it says NOT CHECKED or when the text was CUT. COMPETITOR ANALYSIS may carry a gap that rests on an excerpt of the page ("in the excerpt provided", "not visible in the text provided"): do not repeat it as a finding.
- In the UAE e-invoicing context accreditation of e-invoicing service providers (ASPs) is run by the Ministry of Finance (the Federal Tax Authority administers the tax). Write "accredited under the Ministry of Finance's e-invoicing framework", never "FTA-accredited", even where the competitors' pages say so.
- Never ask for FAQ schema, FAQPage markup or "an FAQ with schema": Google stopped showing FAQ rich results on 2026-05-07 and this system does not emit FAQPage any more. Ask instead for an FAQ SECTION — question-style headings that mirror the People Also Ask questions, each answered directly in the 40-60 words under it, which is what AI assistants quote. Service, Product, Article, Organization and Breadcrumb schema are still fair to ask for.
- time_to_rank_months is a realistic MONTH count for a new page to reach the top 10, judged against the 2025 base rate (Ahrefs: only 1.74% of new pages reach the top 10 within a year, and 40.8% of those do so within a month) and the site's authority — weeks are never realistic for a page without existing rankings.

